/-
# Quasivarieties generated by finite `p`-groups (Kourovka Notebook Problem 10.9)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 10.9 (A. I. Budkin): let `p` be a prime and
`L_p` the set of all quasivarieties each of which is generated by a finite `p`-group. Is `L_p` a
sublattice of the lattice of all quasivarieties of groups?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.PGroup

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.FinitePGroupQuasivarietiesSublatticeKourovka10Problem9Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 10.9 (A. I.
Budkin). UnsolvedMath record UM-00700626 (`KOU-10.9`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

The quasivariety generated by a finite group `K` is the class of groups embeddable in a direct power
of `K` (Mal'cev's `ISPP_u`, and ultrapowers of a finite group are isomorphic to it); classes are
compared on groups in `Type`. Meets are intersections. The join of the quasivarieties generated by
`G` and `H` consists of the groups embeddable in a product of a power of `G` and a power of `H`, and
is generated by `G × H`, so joins stay in `L_p` and the question is about meets. The prime is
universally quantified, so the refutation says that `L_p` is not a sublattice for some `p`.
-/

/-- **Quasivariety generated by a finite group.** For a group $K$, a group $X$ lies in the class
$Q(K)$ when $X$ embeds in a direct power of $K$: there are a set $I$ and an injective
homomorphism $X \to K^I$.

For a finite group $K$, the only case used, $Q(K)$ is the quasivariety generated by $K$, since
ultrapowers of a finite group are isomorphic to it. -/
def InQuasivariety (K : Type) [Group K] (X : Type) [Group X] : Prop :=
  ∃ (I : Type) (f : X →* (I → K)), Function.Injective f

/-- **Join of the quasivarieties generated by two finite groups.** For groups $G$ and $H$, a group
$X$ lies in the class $Q(G, H)$ when $X$ embeds in the product of a direct power of $G$ and a
direct power of $H$: there are sets $I$ and $J$ and an injective homomorphism $X \to G^I \times
H^J$.

For finite groups $G$ and $H$, $Q(G, H)$ is the join of the quasivarieties generated by $G$ and by
$H$, that is, the quasivariety generated by both. -/
def InQuasivarietyJoin (G H : Type) [Group G] [Group H] (X : Type) [Group X] : Prop :=
  ∃ (I J : Type) (f : X →* (I → G) × (J → H)), Function.Injective f

/-- **Kourovka Problem 10.9 (Budkin): quasivarieties generated by finite $p$-groups form a
sublattice.** For every prime $p$ and all finite $p$-groups $G$ and $H$, there is a finite
$p$-group $K$ such that a group lies in the quasivariety generated by $K$ exactly when it lies
in both the quasivariety generated by $G$ and the quasivariety generated by $H$, and there is a
finite $p$-group $K'$ such that a group lies in the quasivariety generated by $K'$ exactly when
it lies in the join of the quasivarieties generated by $G$ and by $H$. Equivalently, the set
$L_p$ of quasivarieties generated by a single finite $p$-group is a sublattice of the lattice of
all quasivarieties of groups. -/
def finitePGroupQuasivarietiesSublattice : Prop :=
  ∀ p : ℕ, p.Prime → ∀ (G H : Type) [Group G] [Finite G] [Group H] [Finite H],
    IsPGroup p G → IsPGroup p H →
    (∃ (K : Type) (_ : Group K) (_ : Finite K), IsPGroup p K ∧
      ∀ (X : Type) [Group X], InQuasivariety K X ↔ (InQuasivariety G X ∧ InQuasivariety H X)) ∧
    (∃ (K : Type) (_ : Group K) (_ : Finite K), IsPGroup p K ∧
      ∀ (X : Type) [Group X], InQuasivariety K X ↔ InQuasivarietyJoin G H X)

/--
**Quasivarieties generated by finite $p$-groups need not form a sublattice.** For some prime $p$
there are finite $p$-groups $G$ and $H$ such that no finite $p$-group generates the intersection
of the quasivarieties generated by $G$ and by $H$, or no finite $p$-group generates their join.
This answers Kourovka Problem 10.9 (Budkin) in the negative.

Since the join is always generated by the finite $p$-group $G \times H$, it is the intersection that
fails to be generated by a finite $p$-group.

Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 10.9 (A. I.
Budkin); resolution: S. A. Shakhova, Math. Notes 53, no. 3 (1993), 345-347, Kourovka Archive,
Problem 10.9 ("No, not always").
-/
public theorem_wanted finitePGroupQuasivarietiesSublattice_refuted :
    ¬ finitePGroupQuasivarietiesSublattice

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.FinitePGroupQuasivarietiesSublatticeKourovka10Problem9Wanted
